7592186262376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186262076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186262088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262376 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262376 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262376 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186262076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262376 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8edf0788-7859-57f4-ac13-2b85b5bf5d5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262630 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262630 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262630 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,i+({x}^{2}-y)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262630 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b1f3451-5b4c-525b-84a2-e17f8516fc90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262630 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)) \\\\  & =2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186438957 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186266079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186438958 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186266079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186438959 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186266079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186438960 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186266079 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266079 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266079 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/17/1709a94b7361a1e8764d94199f9e84802ae925a09e6481250d0846b83bb6549f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- **III.** $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n- **IV.** $F$ is irrotational" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186266079 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a88235e-0bbc-5c38-af1d-92f6a6eda26f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266079 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/7c/7c831425eae7afd709d546a8e05100f495adc04bba6450cfa56d370b415a28f5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1045 "The Curl of a Vector Field" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186409612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409590 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e5b5f674-7783-5eb4-b089-af373cffcaf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409591 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409591 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409591 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fd5ff55-9267-592c-8f53-54d27c28ddbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409595 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409595 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409595 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "613b73ad-b4bd-580f-91d8-dabafde0761e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409596 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409596 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409596 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "64ee45f8-90e8-584e-9ee0-f763b1794d3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409598 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" #edb/ref 1759218604541